{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And Moses said, etc. — Shall your brothers go, etc., and why will you discourage, etc.</b> Behold, our master Moses our teacher perceived in the words of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben everything they intended to say, as we explained. He raised a counter-argument against what they said — that they are requesting from the property of the Most High, for the congregation did nothing — and also against what they intended to claim regarding the future, that there is no toil in the matter, for HaShem alone performs great wonders. He said to them: ״הַאַחֵיכֶם יָבוֹאוּ לַמִּלְחָמָה״ — meaning, it is true that HaShem is the one who wages war for them, but in any case they must be present at the battle. This is what he was precise about: <b>יָבוֹאוּ לַמִּלְחָמָה</b> — and he did not say ״הַאַחֵיכֶם יִלָּחֲמוּ״, to say that he is asking only about the 'bringing,' i.e., their presence. And this contains a refutation both of the claim regarding the war that has already passed and also of the claim regarding the coming war in the land — for in any case every man of Israel has exertion in the matter, so why should they seek the toil of Israel's hands? And he concluded: <b>וְאַתֶּם תֵּשְׁבוּ פֹה</b> — he was shrewd, saying that even though the promise of HaShem is attained effortlessly, since the word of the Most High is like the delivery of an ordinary person and involves no exertion, still you should consider that the coming of Israel to the battle is the equivalent of your sitting here, and you will see that there is a felt effort in coming to the battle. If so, why do you wish to take something that is the exertion of Israel, since you are not toiling with them in your coming to the war of the land?" "<b>Also</b>, he intended in the phrase <b>תֵּשְׁבוּ פֹה</b> with an audible argument: for the land of Sihon and Og, which they are requesting, HaShem gave when the tribes of Israel were gathered together, so that the merit of all of them stood to bequeath this land to them. And when they take their portion in the land, it detracts from the aggregate merit of the multitude, because they are sitting here. And by this he refuted their claim that we understood in the phrase אֲשֶׁר הִכָּה HaShem — which speaks of the wars of the land and that there is no toil for Israel, as I explained above — for it is not so; the matter is not comparable."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:16:38.368682449Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}